absl derives ABSL_HAVE_MMAP from a platform list, and refuses to take the
macro from the command line (`#error ABSL_HAVE_MMAP cannot be directly set`).
The list names __EMSCRIPTEN__ and __asmjs__ but not bare __wasm__, which is
correct upstream -- a bare wasm target has no OS underneath it. Here it does:
this is a musl userland over a kernel that serves mmap/munmap, and the absl
target must additionally build with -U__linux__ so gyp's own libuv source
selection and absl's arch probes can disagree.

Without the macro, low_level_alloc.h sets ABSL_LOW_LEVEL_ALLOC_MISSING, whose
#ifndef swallows the whole of low_level_alloc.cc, graphcycles.cc,
create_thread_identity.cc and per_thread_sem.cc. mutex.cc has no matching
guard and calls into all four regardless, so each became an unresolved import
with a trap-on-call stub and the first absl::Mutex that had to block would
have killed the process.

The DirectMmap path this macro can otherwise select stays unreachable:
low_level_alloc.h defines ABSL_LOW_LEVEL_ALLOC_ASYNC_SIGNAL_SAFE_MISSING for
__wasm__, so the arena allocates through plain mmap/munmap.

--- a/deps/v8/third_party/abseil-cpp/absl/base/config.h
+++ b/deps/v8/third_party/abseil-cpp/absl/base/config.h
@@ -378,7 +378,8 @@
     defined(__asmjs__) || defined(__EMSCRIPTEN__) || defined(__Fuchsia__) || \
     defined(__sun) || defined(__myriad2__) || defined(__HAIKU__) ||          \
     defined(__OpenBSD__) || defined(__NetBSD__) || defined(__QNX__) ||       \
-    defined(__VXWORKS__) || defined(__hexagon__) || defined(__XTENSA__)
+    defined(__VXWORKS__) || defined(__hexagon__) || defined(__XTENSA__) ||   \
+    defined(__wasm__)
 #define ABSL_HAVE_MMAP 1
 #endif
 
